docs {
  # Clinical Co-Pilot API collection, Week 2

  The Week 2 endpoints and workflows: a lab PDF attached to a chart, extracted
  by the sidecar with every value anchored to the page, surfacing as cited
  facts in the briefing; a treatment question answered with guideline
  evidence; the same actions refused without a CSRF token and for a user
  without the documents permission; and the pre-warm status endpoint. Week 1's
  collection (../../clinical_copilot_week1/api-collection) covers the chat
  path in depth; this one is independent of it.

  Run top to bottom ("Run collection"). Requests 3-5 establish the admin
  session on a quiet demo patient (docPid) and capture the CSRF token; 6-11
  are the document workflow; 12 is the CSRF refusal; 13-16 start a second
  session as a restricted user; 17 is the pre-warm status.

   1. Health                      liveness, no auth
   2. Ready                       dependency probes, no auth
   3. Login page                  starts the OpenEMR session
   4. Login                       authenticates (admin by default)
   5. Open demo chart             sets the session patient; captures csrf_token
   6. Documents: list             what is attached to this chart (may be empty)
   7. Documents: upload lab PDF   201 stored, or 200 existing on a re-run; captures document_id
   8. Documents: extract          the sidecar graph runs; anchored results persisted; handoffs returned
   9. Documents: list again       the document reads "extracted" with its confidence
  10. Brief                       facts now carry document citations (page, bounding box)
  11. Ask a treatment question    answer with guideline passages cited by id, and the routing log
  12. Upload without CSRF         403
  13-15. Restricted user          new session, login, open the same chart
  16. Restricted documents list   403 "not authorized to manage documents"
  17. Pre-warm status             enabled flag and the last sweep's counts, no auth

  Select the `local` or `vps` environment. The collection captures the
  session cookie itself (run the CLI with --disable-cookies). The upload is
  idempotent per patient (same bytes = same document), so the collection can
  be run repeatedly; it never deletes what it attached.
}
